Decontamination asks a narrow question: does any training example contain a long verbatim stretch of an eval item? The standard tool is n-gram overlap. GPT-3 used 13-grams. The method is simple. The details decide what it catches.

**What to check against.** Build n-gram sets from the prompt text, the reference or canonical solution and the test code of every eval item: MBPP test (500), the 165 plus-compatible MBPP+ test items, HumanEval+ (163) and dev (200). Check every pool example's completion. Also check the train prompts themselves against test, because MBPP may contain near-duplicate tasks across splits (verify on your data).

**Normalize, then tokenize one fixed way.** Comments, docstrings and formatting are not content. Parse with `ast`, drop docstrings, and let `ast.unparse` re-emit canonical code, which also drops comments and normalizes whitespace and quotes. Then split into identifiers, numbers and single punctuation characters.

```python
import ast, re

_TOK = re.compile(r"[A-Za-z_]\w*|\d+|\S")

def normalize(code: str) -> str:
    try:
        tree = ast.parse(code)
    except SyntaxError:
        return re.sub(r"#[^\n]*", "", code)          # fallback: strip comments only
    for node in ast.walk(tree):
        body = getattr(node, "body", None)
        if (isinstance(body, list) and body and isinstance(body[0], ast.Expr)
                and isinstance(body[0].value, ast.Constant) and isinstance(body[0].value.value, str)):
            node.body = body[1:] or [ast.Pass()]      # drop the docstring
    return ast.unparse(tree)

def ngrams(code: str, n: int = 13) -> set[tuple[str, ...]]:
    toks = _TOK.findall(normalize(code))
    return {tuple(toks[i:i + n]) for i in range(len(toks) - n + 1)}

def contaminated(example: str, eval_grams: set, whitelist: set) -> int:
    return len((ngrams(example) - whitelist) & eval_grams)   # hits, after boilerplate removal
```

**A measured example.** Take an 8-line `first_repeated_char(s)` solution (the MBPP train task the student's week-0 samples solve with a `seen` set) with a docstring and a comment. After normalization it is 31 tokens, so it has $31 - 13 + 1 = 19$ distinct 13-grams. A copy with different indentation, spacing and comments shares all 19. The same program with `seen` renamed to `visited` and `char` renamed to `c` shares **zero**. That is the method's main blind spot, measured.

**Why 13.** A sequence of $T$ tokens has $T - n + 1$ n-grams. Larger $n$ means fewer accidental matches, but a function shorter than $n$ tokens cannot be checked at all, and many MBPP solutions are only 20-60 tokens. Smaller $n$ catches more copies but collides on idioms. Under this tokenizer `for i in range(len(arr)):` is already 11 tokens, so any 13-gram that spans that idiom plus two more common tokens appears in many unrelated programs.

**Flag rule and false positives.** Flag a pool example if it shares any non-whitelisted 13-gram with an eval item. Boilerplate such as `if __name__ == "__main__":` blocks and standard loops will collide. Build a whitelist of generic 13-grams, e.g. those that occur in three or more distinct eval items, and subtract it before counting hits. Alternatively flag on an overlap fraction $|G(x) \cap G_{\text{eval}}| / |G(x)|$ above a threshold. Either way, look at every flagged pair by eye; on this data there will be few.

**What it cannot catch:**

- renamed variables (shown above);
- paraphrased problem statements;
- different but equivalent solutions to a test problem;
- the teacher having memorized MBPP or HumanEval during pretraining. No text check on your pool can see this. The only hint is a suspicious gap between performance on these benchmarks and a fresh set.

You can canonicalize identifiers (replace every non-keyword name with `ID`) to catch renames, at the cost of more false positives. If you do, report both variants.

**Audit it with a planted test.** Insert one verbatim test solution and one renamed copy into a toy pool and run the script. The verbatim one must be flagged. The renamed one will be missed by the plain variant, and you write that down. Then report hit counts **per arm** against each eval set, and list the removed items. Arms can differ in contamination: failure-targeted prompts may be the ones closest to test problems.
